1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:07,320
Unrepresentative Democracy Your government spends six hundred and four times more on weapons than on testing which medicines

2
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:14,900
work. You could elect five hundred and thirty-five Gandhis and within two years they'd be funding nuclear weapons programs,

3
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:21,960
because the system requires it. Princeton studied one thousand, seven hundred and seventy-nine of your policy decisions

4
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:26,540
and found that public opinion has the same effect on legislation as a houseplant.

5
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:34,660
This chapter explains why you can't vote your way out, and why that's actually fine, because the solution was never better

6
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:40,740
politicians. Correlation between public opinion and policy outcomes: zero percent.

7
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:45,760
On Wishonia, decisions are made by whoever has the best evidence.

8
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:53,100
On Earth, decisions are made by whoever has the most papers to give the people who survive a popularity contest held every

9
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:56,540
four years among people who were too busy to research the contestants.

10
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:04,420
You call this "democracy," which is Greek for "rule by the people," in the same way that "waterboarding" is about water.

11
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:10,740
Your chance of deciding a presidential election is one in sixty million.

12
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:19,260
You are more likely to die driving to the polls. The expected value of your vote: zero point zero zero zero three three

13
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:27,220
eight dollars. The cost of casting it (time, transportation, parking, standing in line behind a man arguing about his I.D.,

14
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:35,160
a woman who brought her dog, and a voting machine manufactured by a company that donates to one of the candidates): about

15
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:45,180
fifty dollars. You lose money voting. This is called "civic duty," which means "a financial loss you perform in formal clothing.".

16
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:51,340
Corporations spend four point four billion dollars annually on lobbying.

17
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:56,500
Their math works fine. What Democracy Looks Like From Space.

18
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:03,380
Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred and seventy-nine policy decisions over twenty years.

19
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:10,020
The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually becomes law: zero percent.

20
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:18,600
Not "low." Not "disappointing." Zero. Your democracy correlates with your preferences the way a coin flip correlates with

21
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:24,600
your breakfast. When economic elites want something, it happens seventy-eight percent of the time.

22
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:31,960
When you want something, it happens thirty percent of the time, regardless of whether zero percent or one hundred percent

23
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:38,160
of you want it. The line is flat. A graph of your political influence looks like an E.K.G.

24
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:43,600
of a dead person. You have the same political influence as your houseplant.

25
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:49,580
Less, actually. Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

26
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:56,180
Your institutions already know this. You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

27
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:00,460
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

28
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:05,720
And a "Department of Justice" that... well. You named your planet dirt.

29
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:12,320
Naming is not your strength. The Mathematics of Why Nobody Fixes Anything.

30
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:21,100
Concentrated Benefits vs. Diffuse Costs. Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year.

31
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:28,940
That's ten dollars per person. Will you take a day off work, drive to your capitol, and stand in a hallway to save ten dollars?

32
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:34,460
No. Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion dollars?

33
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:41,240
Obviously. That's it. That is the entire explanation for why your government does what it does.

34
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:47,540
One paragraph. Everything else in this chapter is details. The details are where the crime hides.

35
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:55,200
On Wishonia, we call this "beige crime": theft so boring that nobody investigates it.

36
00:03:55,240 --> 00:04:02,240
Your best criminals discovered long ago that the boredom threshold of the average citizen is approximately ten dollars.

37
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:09,740
Steal more than that from one person and they'll complain. Steal ten dollars from three hundred and thirty million people

38
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:18,260
and you'll receive an industry award. The pattern repeats everywhere, like wallpaper in a prison: Pharma receives five hundred

39
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:25,660
billion dollars in profits (concentrated), patients pay one thousand five hundred dollars each (diffuse), and the winner

40
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:35,700
is pharma (obviously). Banks in two thousand and eight received a seven hundred billion dollar bailout (concentrated), taxpayers

41
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:42,440
paid two thousand one hundred dollars each (diffuse), and the winner is banks (obviously).

42
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:52,500
Sugar receives three point five billion dollars in subsidies (concentrated), you pay ten dollars (not worth a phone call),

43
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:59,660
and the winner is sugar (obviously). The small group hires lobbyists.

44
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:09,140
You hire nobody. This is called "representative democracy," which represents you in the same way a casino represents financial

45
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:16,900
planning. The Death Spiral: How Two Choices Kill three hundred and thirty million Options.

46
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:27,760
A human named James Surowiecki proved that crowds outperform experts under four conditions: diversity of opinion, independence

47
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:33,600
of judgment, decentralization, and a good aggregation mechanism.

48
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:42,780
On Wishonia, we learned this by watching fish. Your political scientists spent two hundred years and several revolutions

49
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:50,620
to reach the same conclusion. The fish were faster. Your democracy has three of these conditions.

50
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:59,380
It systematically destroys the most important one. A human named Charles Mackay described the result in eighteen forty-one:

51
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:06,100
the wisdom of crowds becomes the madness of crowds. He was ignored, obviously.

52
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:12,880
Independence means each person decides based on their own knowledge, not by watching everyone else.

53
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:18,840
Your voting system annihilates this. You have three hundred and thirty million people.

54
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:27,420
You choose between two of them, pre-selected by a process optimized for fundraising, name recognition, and not offending

55
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:33,740
people who vote in primaries (which is approximately nine people per state, seven of whom are angry).

56
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:39,500
Then you don't even vote for the one you like. You vote against the one you fear.

57
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:45,160
This is called "strategic voting," which means your ballot doesn't measure what you want.

58
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:51,100
It measures what you think other people will do. That is not independence.

59
00:06:51,820 --> 00:06:57,900
That is a herd. On Wishonia, we observed this same phenomenon in army ants.

60
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:06,000
Army ants are nearly blind. They navigate by following pheromone trails laid by the ants ahead of them.

61
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:12,680
When a foraging column gets separated from the main trail, the leading ants have nothing to follow, so they wander.

62
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:20,780
But they're still laying pheromone as they walk. If the front of the column curves enough to cross its own trail, the leaders

63
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:30,380
pick up that signal and follow it. The circle closes. Every ant is now both following and reinforcing the path that traps

64
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:37,680
it. They march in a loop, sometimes hundreds of thousands strong, until they die of exhaustion.

65
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:45,440
No individual ant is stupid. Each is following a perfectly rational rule: follow the strongest signal.

66
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:55,440
The system is stupid. The circle emerges not from bad ants but from a structure where no ant can navigate independently.

67
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:59,560
Your two-party system is a death spiral with bumper stickers.

68
00:08:00,260 --> 00:08:05,460
Pollsters tell you who's "electable." Media covers the two "real" candidates.

69
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:13,620
Donors fund the two "viable" options. You vote for one of them because everyone else is voting for one of them because everyone

70
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:20,980
else is voting for one of them. The best candidate out of three hundred and thirty million people could be standing right

71
00:08:20,980 --> 00:08:28,360
there, but if they're not wearing the right color tie, they're "throwing their vote away" (a phrase that only makes sense

72
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:36,480
in a system designed to waste votes). The result: a nation of three hundred and thirty million, selecting from a talent

73
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:45,240
pool of three hundred and thirty million, reliably produces a choice between two people that approximately nobody is enthusiastic

74
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:49,820
about. On Wishonia, this would be classified as a math error.

75
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:55,840
On Earth, it is classified as "freedom." Each ant follows the one ahead.

76
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:00,160
The leader follows the last. They march in a circle until they die.

77
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:05,460
Your two-party system works the same way, but the circle takes four years.

78
00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:15,800
(Clemzouzou sixty-nine, C.C. B.Y. S.A. four point zero). How Money Buys Your Government On most planets, giving someone

79
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:22,080
papers so they'll do what you want is called "bribery." On Earth, it's called "campaign finance."

80
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:29,200
The distinction matters, but not in the way you think. Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

81
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:36,020
Only one in four studies even support that. The system is much more elegant than direct bribery.

82
00:09:36,620 --> 00:09:44,140
What money actually buys is which politicians exist in the first place: Ninety-five percent of House races since two thousand

83
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:52,380
and four were won by whoever had more papers. The average winning House campaign costs two point seven nine million dollars

84
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:56,880
(less than a nice apartment in Manhattan, and arguably less useful).

85
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:05,240
The average winning Senate campaign costs twenty-six point five three million dollars (a senator costs more, presumably

86
00:10:05,240 --> 00:10:12,460
because there are fewer of them, like truffles). Why bribe a politician when you can manufacture one who already agrees

87
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:19,270
with you? On Wishonia, we had a saying: "why train the dog when you can buy a dog that already sits?".

88
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:27,960
For the ones already sitting, papers buy something more useful than votes: Access: Your call gets returned.

89
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:32,280
Everyone else gets voicemail. (Voicemail is where democracy goes to die.).

90
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:37,740
Agenda control: Your issue gets a hearing. Everyone else's gets a filing cabinet.

91
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:44,380
Legislative language: The exact wording that creates the exact loopholes you need.

92
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:52,600
Committee positions: Four hundred and fifty thousand dollars to party committees buys a seat where bills live or die.

93
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:58,610
You don't buy the vote. You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

94
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:06,340
Then the vote takes care of itself. This is like rigging a horse race by buying the horse, the jockey, the track, and the

95
00:11:06,340 --> 00:11:15,900
concept of racing. The Lobbying Return on Investment, or R.O.I.: Better Than Every Other Investment The most profitable

96
00:11:15,900 --> 00:11:20,760
investment on your planet is not stocks, real estate, or cryptocurrency.

97
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:26,880
It's congressmen: Disease advocacy groups give one hundred million dollars in papers to lobbyists.

98
00:11:27,540 --> 00:11:31,300
They receive one point eight billion dollars in papers from the government.

99
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:38,220
This represents a return of eighteen to one (one thousand seven hundred percent net).

100
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:44,780
Your stock market returns about ten percent per year. Your congressmen return seventeen hundred percent.

101
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:52,040
This is not a metaphor. These are the actual numbers. If your stockbroker offered you seventeen hundred percent, you'd call

102
00:11:52,040 --> 00:11:56,060
the police. When your congressman offers it, you call it "advocacy."

103
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:03,460
The only reason more people don't invest in congressmen is that the minimum buy-in requires a trade association, which requires

104
00:12:03,460 --> 00:12:11,840
being rich (which is the entire point). The eighteen-to-one return means your disease funding is allocated by lobbying power,

105
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:18,720
not by who's dying. Diseases with celebrity spokespeople and well-funded advocacy groups get research budgets.

106
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:24,480
Diseases that kill quietly, in populations too poor or too young to organize, get nothing.

107
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:29,300
If your disease can't hire a lobbying firm, it isn't a real disease.

108
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:37,760
It's just dying, which doesn't have a PAC. Your Congressman: A Professional Fundraiser Who Occasionally Dabbles in Legislation.

109
00:12:38,660 --> 00:12:41,600
I studied how your elected representatives spend their time.

110
00:12:42,020 --> 00:12:51,420
I assumed they spent it representing. I was incorrect. How congressmen spend a ten-hour workday: They spend four to five

111
00:12:51,420 --> 00:12:57,160
hours calling rich people for papers. Committee hearings, floor votes, and meetings take four hours.

112
00:12:57,720 --> 00:13:01,920
Reading the laws they're about to vote on gets whatever's left (roughly one hour).

113
00:13:02,660 --> 00:13:05,960
The laws that need reading require six hours' worth of time.

114
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:13,620
That last part is important. The one hundred and tenth Congress dealt with seven thousand, four hundred and forty-one bills

115
00:13:13,620 --> 00:13:20,680
totaling one hundred and twenty-five thousand pages. The House was in session two hundred and ninety-four days.

116
00:13:21,340 --> 00:13:24,480
That is four hundred and twenty-five pages per working day.

117
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:29,120
At normal human reading speed, that takes six hours. They have one.

118
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:37,160
They would need to read at six times normal speed. On Wishonia, we have a word for creatures that process information six

119
00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:40,660
times faster than their biology allows. The word is "lying."

120
00:13:42,140 --> 00:13:49,460
They do not read what they vote on. But it gets worse (on Earth, it always gets worse): they often do not write what they

121
00:13:49,460 --> 00:13:54,580
vote on, either. In twenty thirteen, the House passed a bill to weaken financial regulations.

122
00:13:55,300 --> 00:14:00,020
Of its eighty-five lines, seventy were written by Citigroup lobbyists.

123
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:05,630
Two paragraphs were copied word for word; the only change was making two words plural.

124
00:14:06,140 --> 00:14:11,680
The bank wrote a law to deregulate itself, handed it to Congress, and Congress passed it.

125
00:14:12,280 --> 00:14:17,080
This is like letting the burglar write the building codes. (On your planet, this actually happened.

126
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:25,060
I keep having to clarify this.). Over eight years, at least ten thousand bills copied from corporate templates were introduced

127
00:14:25,060 --> 00:14:29,440
in state legislatures. More than twenty-one hundred became law.

128
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:35,460
Corporations draft the language, hand it to legislators, and legislators hit paste.

129
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:41,300
On Wishonia, we have a word for a system where the person holding the stamp provides no function except the stamp.

130
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:45,800
The word is "rubber." On Earth, the word is "representative."

131
00:14:46,580 --> 00:14:51,720
The Patriot Act: three hundred and forty-two pages, voted on forty-five minutes after release.

132
00:14:52,420 --> 00:15:00,240
That is seven point six pages per minute. The Affordable Care Act: two thousand seven hundred pages, described by the Speaker

133
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:03,300
of the House as something "we have to pass to see what's in it".

134
00:15:04,060 --> 00:15:10,520
On Wishonia, if you approved a two thousand seven hundred-page document without reading it, you would not be allowed to

135
00:15:10,580 --> 00:15:14,880
operate heavy machinery. On Earth, these people operate nuclear weapons.

136
00:15:15,720 --> 00:15:18,300
(And they didn't read the manual for those either, presumably.).

137
00:15:19,400 --> 00:15:24,820
Congressional Committees: The Menu. Committees control which bills live or die.

138
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:31,620
Both parties assign fundraising quotas for each seat: raise this many papers for the party, or lose your assignment.

139
00:15:32,340 --> 00:15:38,600
Don't hit your number? Your bills die, your amendments vanish, and your name goes on a literal "wall of shame".

140
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:47,440
(This is real. I checked twice.). Here is the menu. It has prices, like a restaurant, except the food is legislation, the

141
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:52,080
waiters had to buy their own sections, and the kitchen is run by whoever tips the most.

142
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:59,820
The House Speaker must raise thirty-one million dollars for Democrats or twenty million dollars for Republicans, and in

143
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:08,140
exchange controls the entire legislative agenda. A chair on Ways and Means or Appropriations costs one point eight million

144
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:14,260
dollars and buys control over all tax law or the one point seven trillion dollar federal budget.

145
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:19,860
Energy and Commerce, which controls healthcare, energy, and telecom, costs the same.

146
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:24,600
A regular committee seat runs five hundred thousand to a million dollars.

147
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:32,780
Even the basic entry fee for a freshly elected member is one hundred and fifty thousand dollars, which is rent on a building

148
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:38,240
you were elected to enter. This is why congressmen spend four hours a day fundraising.

149
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:45,840
They're not campaigning. They're paying rent. The people writing your healthcare laws bought their positions from the industries

150
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:51,180
those laws regulate. On your planet, this is called "the system working.".

151
00:16:51,880 --> 00:17:00,120
On Wishonia, this is called "the system.". Public Choice Theory (Or: The Nobel Prize for Cynicism).

152
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:08,420
In nineteen eighty-six, a human named James Buchanan won what is technically not a Nobel Prize (see central banks chapter)

153
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:17,500
in Economics for formally proving something that every organism on every planet already knew: HUMANS DO NOT BECOME BETTER

154
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:22,359
HUMANS WHEN YOU GIVE THEM A GOVERNMENT I.D. BADGE. This is Public Choice Theory.

155
00:17:23,079 --> 00:17:27,700
Bureaucrats, politicians, and regulators are exactly as selfish as everyone else.

156
00:17:28,180 --> 00:17:32,480
The only difference is that when a corporation is greedy, you can stop buying its product.

157
00:17:33,180 --> 00:17:37,420
When a government agency is greedy, it can arrest you for not buying its product.

158
00:17:38,120 --> 00:17:41,760
(This is called "regulation," which means "mandatory purchase.").

159
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:51,460
What Each Institution Actually Optimizes For. On Wishonia, we noticed a pattern: any organism that controls its own food

160
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:58,240
supply eventually optimizes for feeding itself. The surprise is not that your institutions do this.

161
00:17:58,700 --> 00:18:06,240
The surprise is that you keep expecting them not to. Nobody in this system is incentivized to cure disease or end war.

162
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:14,940
You could elect five hundred and thirty-five clones of your most idealistic human and within two years they'd be collecting

163
00:18:14,940 --> 00:18:23,520
papers from pharmaceutical companies, because the system requires it and the system does not care who you put inside it.

164
00:18:24,240 --> 00:18:32,140
It digests idealists the way a stomach digests food. The Misdiagnosis.

165
00:18:32,180 --> 00:18:40,640
Every political faction on your planet has diagnosed the same problem and prescribed a different wrong solution: I attended

166
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:45,560
three of your political meetings. At the first, they said the government was too big.

167
00:18:46,040 --> 00:18:52,760
At the second, they said the government was too small. At the third, they said the government was exactly the right size

168
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:56,720
but full of the wrong species. I left with the third group.

169
00:18:57,440 --> 00:19:04,600
They had better snacks, and also they were correct. The third group is called "Public Choice Theory."

170
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:10,420
Their solution: design systems where selfish behavior accidentally produces good outcomes.

171
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:17,180
On Wishonia, we went with this option four thousand, two hundred and ninety-seven years ago.

172
00:19:17,740 --> 00:19:23,980
It took us twelve minutes to agree. You've been arguing about the first two for several centuries.

173
00:19:24,940 --> 00:19:32,320
This is called "political philosophy," which is the art of being wrong about the same thing in increasingly sophisticated

174
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:38,480
ways. Regulatory Capture: How the Burglar Trained the Guard Dog.

175
00:19:39,240 --> 00:19:44,120
You hired a guard dog. Someone fed it treats. Now it guards them.

176
00:19:44,880 --> 00:19:50,300
There are thirteen thousand, seven hundred registered lobbyists in Washington.

177
00:19:50,340 --> 00:19:59,100
"Lobbying" literally means standing in a lobby. Your corporations spend billions of papers a year paying people to stand

178
00:19:59,100 --> 00:20:04,580
in hallways. On Wishonia, people who stand in hallways are called "lost."

179
00:20:04,980 --> 00:20:08,900
On Earth, they're called "the most powerful people in your government."

180
00:20:09,720 --> 00:20:16,660
Half of them used to work in the government they're now lobbying, which on your planet is called "a career path" and on

181
00:20:16,660 --> 00:20:25,060
Wishonia is called "defecting." Corporations spend four point four billion dollars a year on this professional persuading.

182
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:34,000
Divide that by five hundred and thirty-five members of Congress and you get over eight million dollars in professional persuasion

183
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:39,180
surrounding each congressman, who earns less than a mid-level dentist.

184
00:20:39,920 --> 00:20:44,370
The mystery is not that they're influenced. The mystery is that any of them aren't.

185
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:54,580
A court ruling called Citizens United made corporations "people" who can spend unlimited papers on elections through Super

186
00:20:54,580 --> 00:21:03,380
PACs (which are like normal PACs but super). Unlike you, a corporation cannot be imprisoned, embarrassed, or given cancer.

187
00:21:03,780 --> 00:21:08,160
It has all your rights and none of your problems. This is a good deal if you can get it.

188
00:21:08,900 --> 00:21:14,620
(You can't. You're a person.). Pharmaceutical companies write their own regulations.

189
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:20,180
The Medicare Modernization Act banned the government from negotiating drug prices.

190
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:27,000
This is why insulin that costs six dollars to make costs you three hundred dollars.

191
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:35,440
A four thousand nine hundred percent markup. I checked with your illegal drug dealers, and even they find this excessive.

192
00:21:36,460 --> 00:21:44,820
The Self-Sustaining War Machine. The military-industrial complex distributes operations across forty-two states so that

193
00:21:44,820 --> 00:21:50,840
cutting any program threatens jobs in enough districts to make it politically impossible.

194
00:21:51,480 --> 00:21:58,230
Cutting a weapons program doesn't just lose a contract. It loses votes in forty-two states simultaneously.

195
00:21:59,240 --> 00:22:05,500
This is not an accident. Someone designed this. (Someone good at design, wasted on murder.).

196
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:10,840
But the remarkable part is not that the war industry lobbies for budgets.

197
00:22:11,240 --> 00:22:15,720
It's that the war industry produces the threats that justify the budgets.

198
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:24,410
I had to read this three times because I assumed I was misunderstanding your language: Step zero: Have no enemies.

199
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:33,080
Step one: Build military bases abroad. Step two: Locals resist the foreign military bases (as you would).

200
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:40,500
Step three: Resistance becomes terrorism. Step four: Terrorism justifies military budgets.

201
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:48,260
Step five: Military budgets fund more bases abroad. Step six: Go to step one (forever).

202
00:22:49,640 --> 00:22:56,760
Robert Pape at the University of Chicago found that ninety-five percent of suicide terrorist attacks from nineteen eighty

203
00:22:56,760 --> 00:23:02,720
to two thousand and three were driven by foreign military occupation, not ideology.

204
00:23:03,240 --> 00:23:08,240
Half the attackers were atheists and Marxists who couldn't have found Mecca on a map.

205
00:23:09,100 --> 00:23:17,720
Your eight trillion dollar War on Terror took terrorist attacks from roughly one thousand per year to nearly seventeen thousand

206
00:23:17,720 --> 00:23:25,360
by twenty fourteen. You spent eight trillion dollars to increase the problem by one thousand six hundred percent.

207
00:23:26,180 --> 00:23:29,600
On Wishonia, this is called "watering the fire with gasoline."

208
00:23:30,140 --> 00:23:36,860
On Earth, this is called "defense policy.". Most industries merely buy favorable rules.

209
00:23:37,300 --> 00:23:42,490
The defense industry manufactures the emergencies that make the rules seem necessary.

210
00:23:43,320 --> 00:23:49,240
The Pentagon has never passed an audit. It cannot tell you where the last trillion dollars went.

211
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:58,220
It is, however, very confident about needing the next one. Pharma employs three lobbyists for every member of Congress.

212
00:23:58,260 --> 00:24:03,360
The F.D.A. gets seventy-five percent of its drug review budget from the companies it regulates.

213
00:24:03,920 --> 00:24:08,160
That is like funding the police department with donations from the criminals.

214
00:24:08,880 --> 00:24:15,070
For a detailed examination of what this produces, see F.D.A.: Unsafe and Ineffective.

215
00:24:15,660 --> 00:24:21,960
The Ratchet. The ratchet only turns one way. After World War Two, the U.S.

216
00:24:22,020 --> 00:24:26,300
cut military spending by eighty-seven point six percent in two years.

217
00:24:27,060 --> 00:24:30,460
G.M. went from building bombers back to building Cadillacs.

218
00:24:31,040 --> 00:24:39,320
The economy boomed. It was the greatest period of broad-based prosperity in your country's history, and it happened immediately

219
00:24:39,320 --> 00:24:47,560
after you stopped spending papers on killing people. The military-industrial complex made sure that never happened again.

220
00:24:48,240 --> 00:24:56,720
In inflation-adjusted dollars, peacetime spending is now thirty point six times the pre-World War Two baseline: twenty-nine

221
00:24:56,720 --> 00:25:04,200
billion dollars in nineteen thirty-nine versus eight hundred and eighty-six billion dollars today, in constant dollars,

222
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:12,920
with no world war to justify it. The tumor grew its own blood supply and then reclassified the growth as vital organs.

223
00:25:13,620 --> 00:25:20,280
The Equilibrium. Everything is working exactly as designed: Cures are a one-time payment.

224
00:25:20,900 --> 00:25:27,160
Treatments are recurring revenue. They fund treatments. Approving a bad drug ends careers.

225
00:25:27,700 --> 00:25:32,280
Delaying a good one ends lives, but not careers. They delay drugs.

226
00:25:33,360 --> 00:25:38,680
Everyone who decides things is winning. Everyone who's dying is not deciding things.

227
00:25:39,120 --> 00:25:45,150
The winners keep deciding. The system works perfectly. It is just optimized for the wrong species.

228
00:25:46,060 --> 00:25:49,960
On Wishonia, we noticed the same pattern six thousand years ago.

229
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:57,840
The solution was not to elect better people. (We tried. They got eaten by the system in about twelve minutes.) The solution

230
00:25:57,840 --> 00:26:02,620
was to build a different system. The Solution They Don't Want You to Have.

231
00:26:04,260 --> 00:26:12,320
Lobbying is just one gear. The full cost of political dysfunction (military overspend, healthcare administration, regulatory

232
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:21,120
friction, tax complexity, corporate welfare, and the rest) totals four point nine trillion dollars per year, or seventeen

233
00:26:21,120 --> 00:26:31,020
percent of G.D.P. A forensic audit identifies two point forty-five trillion dollars in recoverable papers per year if U.S.

234
00:26:31,020 --> 00:26:35,380
governance merely matched the average rich country. You don't need a revolution.

235
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:42,720
You need a reallocation. Revolutions require guns. Reallocations require spreadsheets.

236
00:26:43,540 --> 00:26:48,060
You are surprisingly bad at both. But nobody fights to recover it.

237
00:26:48,600 --> 00:26:55,660
Same pattern as this entire chapter: each corporation gains one hundred million dollars (worth hiring a thousand lobbyists).

238
00:26:56,320 --> 00:27:02,520
Each citizen loses thousands (not worth a phone call). The losses are too spread out to organize around.

239
00:27:03,100 --> 00:27:09,720
The gains are too concentrated to give up. But what if curing disease was more profitable than treating it?

240
00:27:10,380 --> 00:27:16,060
What if peace paid better than war? What if you could point the same corruption machine at something useful?

241
00:27:16,980 --> 00:27:20,900
That's what a one percent treaty does. It redirects the papers.

242
00:27:21,300 --> 00:27:28,460
But redirecting papers into a system run by the same committees and lobbyists just creates a new pile for the same parasites

243
00:27:28,460 --> 00:27:35,840
to feed on. You need two more things. Incentive Alignment Bonds turn the bribery machine against itself.

244
00:27:36,960 --> 00:27:41,900
Politicians who vote for the treaty get reelection papers and cushy post-office careers.

245
00:27:42,380 --> 00:27:46,400
Politicians who vote against it watch that same money go to their opponents.

246
00:27:46,980 --> 00:27:50,840
You don't fight the corruption. You outbid it. Same machine.

247
00:27:51,200 --> 00:27:57,600
Same greed. Different output. Wishocracy replaces the committees that lobbyists capture.

248
00:27:58,340 --> 00:28:04,760
Instead of five hundred and thirty-five congressmen deciding which diseases get funded (based on who bought them dinner),

249
00:28:05,400 --> 00:28:10,920
eight billion people make pairwise comparisons, and an algorithm allocates the budget.

250
00:28:11,020 --> 00:28:18,760
No committee chairs. No fundraising quotas. No lobbyist can bribe eight billion random voters, and the only way to try is

251
00:28:18,760 --> 00:28:23,420
to offer them something they actually want (which is the system working correctly, for once).

252
00:28:24,280 --> 00:28:30,060
The plan is to give the bribers more money than the other bribers are giving them, and then replace the thing they were

253
00:28:30,060 --> 00:28:33,900
bribing with an equation. On Wishonia, this took twelve minutes.

254
00:28:34,420 --> 00:28:39,020
On Earth, it will probably take longer, because you will want to argue about it first.
